# 6. Objective Lifecycle

## Purpose

The Objective Lifecycle defines the controlled engineering stages through which every System Objective progresses from its initial proposal to its eventual retirement.

The lifecycle ensures that all objectives are developed, reviewed, approved, implemented, maintained, and retired in a consistent, traceable, and disciplined manner.

---

## Lifecycle Principles

The lifecycle shall:

- Ensure engineering discipline throughout objective development.
- Support complete traceability.
- Preserve historical records.
- Prevent uncontrolled modifications.
- Promote continuous engineering improvement.

---

## Lifecycle Stages

### Stage 1 — Proposed

An engineering need for a new System Objective has been identified but has not yet undergone formal engineering review.

---

### Stage 2 — Draft

The objective has been documented in sufficient detail for engineering evaluation and discussion.

---

### Stage 3 — Under Review

The objective is undergoing technical, engineering, and governance review to assess its necessity, consistency, and alignment with the AVIA engineering framework.

---

### Stage 4 — Approved

The objective has successfully completed the required review process and is authorized for use within the engineering repository.

---

### Stage 5 — Active

The approved objective is in active use and may be referenced by requirements, architectures, analyses, verification, and validation activities.

---

### Stage 6 — Revised

The objective has undergone an approved modification while preserving its engineering history and traceability.

---

### Stage 7 — Deprecated

The objective is no longer recommended for new engineering work but is retained to preserve compatibility and historical traceability.

---

### Stage 8 — Retired

The objective has been formally withdrawn from active use following engineering review. Retired objectives remain archived for historical reference and traceability.

---

## Lifecycle Transitions

Objectives shall only transition between lifecycle stages through the governance processes defined within this document.

Every transition shall:

- Be documented.
- Be justified.
- Preserve traceability.
- Maintain revision history.

---

## Lifecycle Traceability

Each objective shall maintain a record of:

- Current lifecycle stage.
- Previous lifecycle stages.
- Date of transition.
- Reason for transition.
- Approving authority.
- Related revision records.
